WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.892
Numeric input needs two levels of
validation.

2
00:00:02.972 --> 00:00:06.407
First, is this actually a number in the
format the program expects?

3
00:00:06.487 --> 00:00:09.615
Second, is this number acceptable for the
specific use case?

4
00:00:09.695 --> 00:00:13.314
Passing the first question does not
automatically answer the second.

5
00:00:13.394 --> 00:00:16.093
Conversion failure should be detected
explicitly.

6
00:00:16.173 --> 00:00:19.179
Parsing code should know whether no digits
were consumed,

7
00:00:19.259 --> 00:00:21.958
whether the value was outside the
conversion range,

8
00:00:22.038 --> 00:00:25.166
and whether unexpected characters remain
after the number.

9
00:00:25.246 --> 00:00:29.172
A string such as a number followed by
extra text may be valid for one format and

10
00:00:29.252 --> 00:00:31.767
invalid for another, so the rule should be
clear.

11
00:00:31.847 --> 00:00:35.711
Range checks should happen before the
value drives allocation, indexing,

12
00:00:35.791 --> 00:00:39.165
loop counts, timeouts, resource limits, or
protocol decisions.

13
00:00:39.245 --> 00:00:42.802
A number can be syntactically valid and
still be too large, too small,

14
00:00:42.882 --> 00:00:45.643
negative where only nonnegative values are
allowed,

15
00:00:45.723 --> 00:00:47.992
or outside the business rule for that
field.

16
00:00:48.072 --> 00:00:50.771
Signed and unsigned interpretation
deserves care.

17
00:00:50.851 --> 00:00:55.206
Conversions between signed and unsigned
types can change comparisons and boundary

18
00:00:55.286 --> 00:00:55.716
checks.

19
00:00:55.796 --> 00:01:00.151
Code should avoid accepting a value under
one interpretation and then using it under

20
00:01:00.231 --> 00:01:01.519
another without review.

21
00:01:01.599 --> 00:01:04.359
Overflow-aware reasoning continues after
parsing.

22
00:01:04.439 --> 00:01:08.487
A count may be in range by itself but
become unsafe when multiplied by an element

23
00:01:08.567 --> 00:01:11.818
size, added to a header length, or
combined with another field.

24
00:01:11.898 --> 00:01:16.069
Validation should include later
calculations that depend on the numeric value.

25
00:01:16.149 --> 00:01:17.867
Context decides acceptability.

26
00:01:17.947 --> 00:01:21.198
A port number, buffer size, retry count,
array index, timeout,

27
00:01:21.278 --> 00:01:23.118
and record count can all be numeric,

28
00:01:23.198 --> 00:01:25.958
but they have different allowed ranges and
meanings.

29
00:01:26.038 --> 00:01:29.903
Good validation names the intended use
instead of treating all successful

30
00:01:29.983 --> 00:01:31.700
conversions as trusted numbers.
